  "id": "DrugBank_DB08806 - MONDO_1126",
  "verbalization": "Roxatidine Acetate is implicated in the pathogenesis of gastric ulcer through a mechanism involving gastrin secretion. The evidence suggests that Roxatidine Acetate may modulate gastrin secretion, as it is linked to gastrin secretion abnormalities, which in turn are associated with gastric ulceration. This pathway is supported by the presence of two distinct but related indirect pathways: one involving Omeprazole and another involving Famotidine, both of which are known to inhibit gastric acid secretion and are used in the treatment of gastric ulcers. The shared role of gastrin secretion abnormalities in these pathways underscores a potential mechanism by which Roxatidine Acetate could influence gastric ulcer development. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between Roxatidine Acetate and gastric ulcer, particularly through its effect on gastrin secretion, which may contribute to the pathophysiology of the disease. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of gastrin regulation in the context of gastric ulcer, and the potential for Roxatidine Acetate to be considered as a candidate for repositioning in the treatment of gastric ulcers.",
